In early October (after a bunch of early-game *splats* (four of which
are included in the above 5-for-11)), I asked myself, "What's the
difference between MY games that win and those that don't?"  In almost
every case up to that point, playing too quickly (directly or
indirectly) led to YAESD.

I promptly rung up back-to-back ascentions, both of which featured:
- constant fretting over what would go wrong
- excessive panic
- a concious effort to always play cautiously
- Marvin-esque levels of paranoia
- egregious mistakes (I cancelled my (mostly) enchanted AK in both
games :-)
- obscenely good luck
  (1st game: 11 wishes used, with 2 more available)
  (2nd game: 15 wishes used, with 5 more available)
- unnecessary doubt about my own abilities
- a semi-regular audience (at least 2 people that I know of)

The biggest lesson I learned just before/during those two games: It's
not the RNG, it's me.  I'm the one making the mistakes.  I'm the one
playing poorly.  Starting stats don't matter.  Starting inventory
doesn't matter.  Once I'm past T:0, it's 95% skill and 5% luck.

Since finishing that second game:  I start a character EXPECTING to
ascend.  If I don't, I made a mistake somewhere.

